---
import DemoLayout from '../../components/DemoLayout.astro'
import EdgeToc from '@zone/ui/components/EdgeToc.astro'
import SeriesPager from '@zone/ui/components/SeriesPager.astro'
import Callout from '@zone/ui/components/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from '@zone/ui/components/CodeBlock.astro'
import Button from '@zone/ui/components/Button.astro'
import { essayHeadings } from '../../data/demo'

const proseCode = `.z-prose {
  font-family: var(--z-font-prose);
  font-size: 22px;
  line-height: 1.68;
}

:lang(zh), :lang(ja) {
  --z-font-prose: var(--z-font-reading);
}`
---

<DemoLayout
  title="The Quiet Page"
  description="The article demo for Hairline: one centered measure, an edge index, and a serif reading voice."
  current="/demos/archive/"
>
  <div class="shell">
    <article class="mx-auto w-full max-w-reading">
      <header class="mb-8 border-b border-line pb-6">
        <h1 class="mt-0 font-prose text-heading-40 font-semibold text-ink max-sm:text-heading-32">The Quiet Page</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[56ch] font-prose text-lede-24 text-ink-secondary max-sm:text-copy-18">
          What a reading interface owes the reader.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-2 text-label-12 tabular-nums text-ink-tertiary">
          <time datetime="2026-10-22">2026.10.22</time>
          <span class="text-ink-quiet">·</span>
          <span>12 min</span>
          <span class="text-ink-quiet">·</span>
          <Button variant="quiet" data-copy-link class="min-w-[4.75rem]">Copy link</Button>
          <span class="sr-only" data-copy-status aria-live="polite"></span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="z-prose z-prose--numbered" lang="en">
        <p class="z-lede">
          A page is a promise: nothing here is more interesting than the
          sentence you are reading. Most interfaces break that promise before
          the first paragraph. The ones that keep it are not simpler. They are
          more deliberate.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-measure">The measure</h2>
        <p>
          Reading happens in a line, and a line has a length. Too short and the
          eye bounces from one ragged edge to the other; too long and it loses
          its place on the return. The typographic answer is old and
          unglamorous: set a measure, then defend it.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>Typography exists to honor content.</p>
          <cite>Robert Bringhurst</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          A measure of 45 to 75 characters is the usual comfort zone for a
          book.<sup><a href="#fn-1" id="fnref-1" aria-label="Footnote 1">1</a></sup>
          A screen is not a book, but the eye has not changed. What has changed
          is how easily a page can forget the measure while it chases a
          viewport.
        </p>

        <h3 id="a-measure-in-characters">A measure in characters</h3>
        <p>
          A measure in characters is not a width in pixels. It changes with the
          typeface and with the reader's settings. That is the point: the
          column should be defined by the text, not by a device. The old column
          was 68ch of the UI mono: 68 characters of mono, about 80 of 19px
          serif. The new reading measure is 78ch: about 81 characters at 22px.
          The wider column and the larger body were chosen together; one
          without the other would miss<sup><a href="#fn-2" id="fnref-2" aria-label="Footnote 2">2</a></sup>.
        </p>
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Two measures, five faces.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Face</th>
              <th>Size / leading</th>
              <th>Measure</th>
              <th class="text-right tabular-nums">Characters</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Reading</td>
              <td>22 / 1.68</td>
              <td>78ch</td>
              <td class="text-right tabular-nums">≈ 81</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Before</td>
              <td>19 / 1.68</td>
              <td>68ch</td>
              <td class="text-right tabular-nums">≈ 80</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mono (ref.)</td>
              <td>16 / 1.70</td>
              <td>68ch</td>
              <td class="text-right tabular-nums">68</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>CJK</td>
              <td>18 / 1.90</td>
              <td>40em</td>
              <td class="text-right tabular-nums">≈ 40</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Code</td>
              <td>13 / 1.70</td>
              <td>78ch</td>
              <td class="text-right tabular-nums">78</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          At 81 characters the new reading line is at the top of the range; the
          old 19px line was at 80. The 22px body is what keeps the wider column
          readable. The 78ch width alone at 19px would be about 94 characters,
          over the limit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Too often a page sets its width once, in pixels, and then changes the
          type inside it. The column is a container; the text is a guest.
          Reverse it. The column follows the text, and the page follows the
          column.
        </p>
        <p class="font-ui text-label-13"><a class="text-accent underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-[0.25em] hover:decoration-current" href="/demos/measure/">Open the measure lab →</a></p>

        <h2 id="the-margin">The margin</h2>
        <p>
          Margins are not leftover space. They are where the page tells you
          what it is: a date, a series, a language, a chapter number. Put those
          facts at the top of the page, or in the margin, and the reading
          column stays uninterrupted. The reader can look when they need to and
          ignore it when they do not.
        </p>
        <p>
          This page keeps the margin mostly empty. The title block carries the
          facts; the right edge carries a thin index of the sections, invisible
          until you reach for it. There is no sidebar, no sticky card, nothing
          that asks to be looked at while the essay is being read.
        </p>
        <Callout kind="accent">
          <p>
            Chinese and Japanese get a different measure: 18px with 1.9
            leading, 0.75em block gaps, and no negative tracking. The rhythm is
            the same; only the values change.
          </p>
        </Callout>
        <p>
          Small screens have no margin to spare. The edge index leaves, the
          metadata moves under the title, and the article becomes one column.
          Nothing is lost. It is re-sequenced.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-voice">The voice</h2>
        <p>
          Type has a voice before it has a size. IBM Plex Mono is the apparatus
          of this site: dates, labels, numbers, navigation, code. Newsreader is
          the essay itself. The two are deliberately different.
        </p>

        <p>
          A 4,000-word essay in mono is a commitment. The characters are even,
          the rhythm is mechanical, and after twenty minutes the reader is aware
          of the machine. Serif is the publication's normal voice: even color, a
          quiet rhythm, a measure that disappears.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no reading-mode switch. Serif is the reading voice; mono is
          the apparatus. A reader should not have to configure a page before
          reading it.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock code={proseCode} />
        <p>
          In Chinese and Japanese the question does not arise. Mono Latin leaves
          gaps around Han characters, so prose resolves to the proportional
          reading stack at every size. The chrome stays mono.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-weather">The weather</h2>
        <p>
          Dark mode is not a filter. A white page inverted becomes a different
          page; the ink hierarchy collapses; the accent loses its contrast. The
          dark theme is a second set of values, chosen for a different light.
        </p>
        <p>
          A page that is calm in daylight should be calm at night. The dark
          paper is nearly black, the ink is cool and slightly soft, and the
          accent is lighter. The hierarchy survives; only the luminance flips.
        </p>
        <Callout>
          <p>
            The theme is automatic by default. The switch in the corner exists
            to inspect both. A product switch is a promise to maintain a third
            state, not just a toggle.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="a-page-not-a-screen">A page, not a screen</h2>
        <p>
          None of this is visible. That is the test. A reader should remember
          the essay, the argument, the sentence they underlined, and not the
          interface that held it.
        </p>
        <p>
          A quiet page is not an empty page. It is a page where every rule,
          every margin and every type size has already made the decision, so
          the reader does not have to.
        </p>
        <p>
          The best compliment is the one this design is trying to earn: I forgot
          I was reading on a screen.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="z-footnotes" aria-label="Footnotes">        <ol>
          <li id="fn-1">
            Robert Bringhurst, <em>The Elements of Typographic Style</em>, on
            measure and the 45–75 character range.
            <a href="#fnref-1" aria-label="Back to reference 1">↩</a>
          </li>
          <li id="fn-2">
            The old column was 68ch of IBM Plex Mono at 16px; at 19px,
            Newsreader fit about 80 characters. The new reading measure is
            78ch at 22px, about 81; the column and the body changed together.
            A ch is the width of the zero, not the average character.
            <a href="#fnref-2" aria-label="Back to reference 2">↩</a>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <SeriesPager
        previousLabel="Previous essay"
        nextLabel="Next essay"
        previous={{ title: 'The Senior Engineer Death Spiral', href: 'https://shuzhi.zone/en/blog/the-senior-engineer-death-spiral/' }}
        next={{ title: 'The Nature of the Firm', href: 'https://shuzhi.zone/en/blog/the-nature-of-the-firm/' }}
      />
    </article>
  </div>

  <EdgeToc headings={essayHeadings} />
</DemoLayout>

<script>
  const copyButtons = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy-link]'))
  const status = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-copy-status]')

  for (const button of copyButtons) {
    const label = button.textContent ?? 'Copy link'
    button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(window.location.href)
        button.textContent = 'Copied'
        if (status) status.textContent = 'Link copied to the clipboard.'
      } catch {
        button.textContent = 'Copy failed'
        if (status) status.textContent = 'Copy failed. The URL is in the address bar.'
      }
      window.setTimeout(() => {
        button.textContent = label
        if (status) status.textContent = ''
      }, 1800)
    })
  }
</script>
